<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Adams</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1735">1735</a>–<a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Adams was a Founding Father and the second president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, serving from 1797 to 1801 after two terms as its first vice president. A Massachusetts lawyer, he defended British soldiers charged after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> Massacre, and as a delegate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a> helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> draft the Declaration of Independence. He was a diplomat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> during the Revolutionary War and the first United States ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. As president he signed the Alien and Sedition Acts and fought an undeclared naval war with France, losing the election of 1800 to Jefferson. He died in 1826, on the fiftieth anniversary of the Declaration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-adams.jpg" alt="John Adams" width="330" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gilbert Stuart · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gilbert_Stuart,_John_Adams,_c._1800-1815,_NGA_42933.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Even Thomas Jefferson agreed, in a letter to <b>John Adams</b> in 1813, that there was a “natural aristocracy” among men, grounded in “virtue and talents”, although he wanted it chosen by election rather than by birth.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>…republic influenced political writers from James Harrington, who borrowed its ballot for The Commonwealth of Oceana (1656), to Jean-Jacques Rousseau, who served as secretary to the French ambassador in Venice in 1743–1744, and <b>John Adams</b>, who studied it in his defence of the American state constitutions in 1787.</blockquote><a href="/government/oligarchy">Oligarchy · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the original Constitution the runner-up in the electoral college became vice-president, so in 1796 <b>John Adams</b> found his chief rival, Thomas Jefferson, serving as his deputy; the Twelfth Amendment of 1804 ended the arrangement.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>President <b>John Adams</b> signed the act moving the government to Washington and setting aside $5,000 for books for Congress.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-24">24 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Residence Act approved the creation of the capital district of the United States along the Potomac River, considered the founding date of Washington, D.C. Congress moved there from Philadelphia in 1800, and <b>John Adams</b> moved into the new White House.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-16">16 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Adams</b> predicted that 2 July would be celebrated for ever; posterity chose the 4th.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-2">2 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Braintree, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Quincy, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.21,-71.01&amp;year=1794" aria-label="Braintree on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2827 662 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Adams"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3277 932h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3277 932h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.21,-71.01&amp;year=1794">What was here in 1794? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It ought to be solemnized with pomp and parade, with shows, games, sports, guns, bells, bonfires, and illuminations, from one end of this continent to the other, from this time forward forevermore.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Abigail Adams on the vote for independence, 3 July 1776 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/34123/pg34123-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Posterity! you will never know how much it cost the present generation to preserve your freedom! I hope you will make a good use of it.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Abigail Adams, 26 April 1777 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/34123/pg34123-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I must study politics and war, that my sons may have liberty to study mathematics and philosophy.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Abigail Adams from Paris, 1780 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/34123/pg34123-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am well aware of the toil and blood and treasure that it will cost us to maintain this Declaration and support and defend these States. Yet, through all the gloom, I can see the rays of ravishing light and glory.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Abigail Adams, 3 July 1776 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/34123/pg34123-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It seems to be the intention of Heaven that we should be taught the full value of our liberty by the dearness of the purchase, and the importance of public virtue by the necessity of it.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Abigail Adams from Passy, 1778 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/34123/pg34123-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Public virtues and political qualities, therefore, should be incessantly cherished in our children.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Abigail Adams, 29 October 1775 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/34123/pg34123-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
